---
import Artwork from "@/components/Artwork.astro"
import CampaignPartners from "@/components/CampaignPartners.astro"
import CampaignStatus from "@/components/CampaignStatus.astro"
import HistoryStack from "@/components/HistoryStack.astro"
import LegalRoute from "@/components/LegalRoute.astro"
import MetaPage from "@/components/MetaPage.astro"
import StoryScene from "@/components/StoryScene.astro"
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro"
import { campaignStatus } from "@/data/campaign"
---

<Layout>
  <MetaPage slot="head" />
  <article class="campaign landing">
    <header class="hero">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <CampaignStatus language="en" />
        <h1>
          DAC8 records every crypto customer.
          <span>We are fighting back.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Since 2026, exchanges and brokers in Austria have had to prepare a
          yearly record for the tax authority of what you buy, sell, exchange
          and move to your own wallets. For all customers, without suspicion.
        </p>
        <nav class="hero-actions" aria-label="Get started">
          <a class="button primary" href="/en/get-involved/#spenden"
            >Support the case</a
          >
          <a class="button" href="/en/dossier/">What DAC8 requires</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
      <Artwork
        id="collectionReview"
        language="en"
        size="hero"
        priority
        caption="Our planned complaint targets the blanket collection of all customers’ data."
      />
    </header>
    <section class="figures" aria-label="DAC8 in Austria">
      <dl class="keyfigures">
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>Customers</strong>
            are covered, even if nothing is ultimately reported.
          </dt>
          <dd>All</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>Retention</strong>
            of each annual report by the provider.
          </dt>
          <dd>10 <small>years</small></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>First report</strong>
            to the tax authority, for the year 2026.
          </dt>
          <dd><time datetime="2027-07-31">31 July 2027</time></dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>
    <section id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <h2 id="problem-heading">What DAC8 does with your data</h2>
      <div class="story trio">
        <StoryScene
          art="accountToRecord"
          language="en"
          title="Your account becomes a file."
        >
          <p>
            Name, address, tax number and date of birth, next to your annual
            totals per crypto-asset: bought, sold, exchanged and moved to your
            own wallets.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="paragraphWatch"
          language="en"
          title="Everyone recorded. Without suspicion."
        >
          <p>
            Providers prepare this for all customers, including those who will
            not ultimately be reported. Austrian law requires it, going beyond
            the EU directive.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="yearsOnFile"
          language="en"
          title="More about you. Every year."
        >
          <p>
            The provider keeps each annual report for ten years. Your ID, wallet
            addresses and transactions stay for up to ten years after the
            customer relationship ends.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/dossier/#gemeldet"
        >What is reported and what the provider keeps</a
      >
    </section>
    <section id="geschichte" aria-labelledby="geschichte-heading">
      <h2 id="geschichte-heading">How DAC8 came about</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        It began in 1977 with assistance in individual cases. Today banks,
        platforms and crypto providers report every year, more with each step.
      </p>
      <HistoryStack language="en" compact />
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/history/">The full history since 1977</a>
    </section>
    <section id="risiko" aria-labelledby="risiko-heading">
      <h2 id="risiko-heading">What it can lead to</h2>
      <div class="story chain">
        <StoryScene art="dataHack" language="en" title="Stored. Hacked. Sold.">
          <p>
            The more data sits in one place, and the longer, the more a break-in
            pays. Exchanges, identity verification services and tax
            administrations have already lost such data.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="profileFourSources"
          language="en"
          title="Every leak sharpens your profile."
        >
          <p>
            Your face from the web, your wallet from a leak, your holdings from
            a hacked tax service. Together they reveal who you are, where you
            live and what you probably own.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="rankedPortrait"
          language="en"
          title="The more you move, the higher you rank."
        >
          <p>
            In Austria, fraudsters phish for crypto holders with fake tax-office
            messages. A breach at a provider would hand them the list
            ready-made, sorted by volume.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="targetAtHome"
          language="en"
          title="Your profile. Your address. A target."
        >
          <p>
            This is organised crime. In the first half of 2026, the French
            interior ministry counted 77 kidnappings, unlawful detentions and
            extortion cases involving crypto-assets.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/dossier/#problem">Why it is a problem</a>
    </section>
    <section id="faelle" aria-labelledby="faelle-heading">
      <h2 id="faelle-heading">This has already happened</h2>
      <ul class="cases">
        <li>
          <span class="case-kind">Hardware wallet shop</span>
          <strong class="case-figure">272,000</strong>
          <p>
            Buyers’ names and addresses leaked. Extortion threats and letters to
            their homes followed.
          </p>
          <span class="case-source">Ledger, 2020</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="case-kind">Identity verification</span>
          <strong class="case-figure">≈ 6,300</strong>
          <p>
            Users with ID photo, home and wallet address in one record. A ransom
            demand followed.
          </p>
          <span class="case-source">Fractal ID, 2024</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="case-kind">Exchange</span>
          <strong class="case-figure">up to $400m</strong>
          <p>
            Coinbase’s cost estimate after bribed staff passed customer data to
            fraudsters.
          </p>
          <span class="case-source">Coinbase, 2025</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="case-kind">Tax administration</span>
          <strong class="case-figure">1.2m</strong>
          <p>
            Records from the bank-account register, with IBAN, name and address,
            taken with officials’ stolen credentials.
          </p>
          <span class="case-source">France, 2026</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="link-row">
        <a class="text-link" href="/en/attacks/"
          >Attacks on crypto holders worldwide</a
        >
        <a class="text-link" href="/en/dossier/#faelle"
          >More cases and sources</a
        >
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="weg" aria-labelledby="weg-heading">
      <h2 id="weg-heading">What we are doing</h2>
      <div class="columns">
        <p class="section-intro">
          <strong
            >We are convinced that the blanket collection of all customers’ data
            violates fundamental rights.</strong
          >
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          {campaignStatus.en.text}
        </p>
      </div>
      <LegalRoute language="en" />
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/proceedings/">Where the case stands</a>
    </section>
    <section id="handeln" aria-labelledby="handeln-heading">
      <h2 id="handeln-heading">How you can help</h2>
      <div class="act">
        <div class="act-lead">
          <Artwork id="financeTheCase" language="en" caption={false} />
          <div>
            <h3>Help fund the case</h3>
            <p>Your donation pays for the proceedings.</p>
            <a class="button primary" href="/en/get-involved/#spenden"
              >Donate</a
            >
          </div>
        </div>
        <ul class="act-list">
          <li>
            <a href="/en/get-involved/#abgeordnete">
              <strong>Write to your MPs</strong>
              <span
                >A letter to the National Council, drafted in your browser and
                sent by you.</span
              >
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/en/proceedings/">
              <strong>Follow the case</strong>
              <span>We publish news and next steps here and via RSS.</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/en/get-involved/#teilen">
              <strong>Share the campaign</strong>
              <span
                >Ready-made graphics on the story, in German and English.</span
              >
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="vision" class="vision" aria-labelledby="vision-heading">
      <h2 id="vision-heading" class="statement">
        Privacy needs no justification.
        <span>State interference does.</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        Where there is specific suspicion of a crime, authorities should
        investigate that case, with a legal basis and independent oversight. We
        reject collecting everyone’s financial data as a precaution.
      </p>
      <div class="vision-points">
        <div>
          <h3>What we aim to achieve</h3>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>Overturn disproportionate obligations</li>
            <li>Limit data collection</li>
            <li>Secure effective protection for those affected</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="precedent">
          <h3>Why we are confident</h3>
          <p class="precedent-year" aria-hidden="true">2014</p>
          <div>
            <p>
              In 2014, the Court of Justice struck down the data retention
              directive and Austria’s Constitutional Court struck down the
              national law.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="position" aria-labelledby="position-heading">
      <h2 id="position-heading">Who we are</h2>
      <div class="about">
        <div>
          <p class="section-intro">
            dac8.org is an initiative by Bitcoin Austria, the nonprofit Bitcoin
            association founded in Vienna in 2011. We are Bitcoiners and
            cypherpunks. We see privacy as a prerequisite for a free society.
          </p>
          <p class="prose">
            The campaign concerns everyone covered by DAC8, whatever
            crypto-assets they use. It starts in Austria, but DAC8 applies
            across the EU. If you want to challenge the law in your country, get
            in touch:
            <a href="mailto:kontakt@dac8.org">kontakt@dac8.org</a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <CampaignPartners />
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
